"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,562\nLearning to see: faces and words\n\n3\n00:00:09,562 --> 00:00:15,884\nIn 2010, Stanislas Dehaene and his colleagues scanned the brains of 63 adults.\n\n4\n00:00:16,303 --> 00:00:18,465\nSome had learned to read as children,\n\n5\n00:00:18,567 --> 00:00:21,763\nsome as adults, and ten had never learned at all.\n\n6\n00:00:22,257 --> 00:00:27,585\nIn readers, a small patch of the visual brain responded strongly to written words.\n\n7\n00:00:27,948 --> 00:00:29,319\nThe better people read,\n\n8\n00:00:29,524 --> 00:00:31,216\nthe stronger it responded.\n\n9\n00:00:32,386 --> 00:00:34,785\nThis is the visual word form area.\n\n10\n00:00:35,173 --> 00:00:38,308\nIt sits on the underside of the brain,\n\n11\n00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:44,064\non the left, right next to a patch that responds most to faces: the fusiform face area.\n\n12\n00:00:46,234 --> 00:00:49,797\nWriting was invented only about 5,000 years ago,\n\n13\n00:00:50,129 --> 00:00:53,297\ntoo recently for the brain to evolve a reading area.\n\n14\n00:00:53,759 --> 00:00:57,804\nInstead, learning to read recycles part of the visual system.\n\n15\n00:00:58,311 --> 00:01:01,611\nIn six-year-olds scanned through their first year of school,\n\n16\n00:01:01,858 --> 00:01:04,004\nthe word area appeared within months,\n\n17\n00:01:04,116 --> 00:01:05,626\nas their reading took off.\n\n18\n00:01:07,296 --> 00:01:09,431\nFaces get special treatment too.\n\n19\n00:01:09,854 --> 00:01:11,241\nTurn a face upside down,\n\n20\n00:01:11,261 --> 00:01:13,454\nand it becomes much harder to read.\n\n21\n00:01:13,998 --> 00:01:15,345\nIn the Thatcher illusion,\n\n22\n00:01:15,559 --> 00:01:20,446\neyes and a mouth flipped inside an upside-down face look almost normal.\n\n23\n00:01:20,829 --> 00:01:22,218\nTurn the picture upright,\n\n24\n00:01:22,238 --> 00:01:24,031\nand the face looks grotesque.\n\n25\n00:01:26,701 --> 00:01:33,026\nAround two in every hundred people are thought to have lifelong trouble recognizing faces,\n\n26\n00:01:33,355 --> 00:01:35,728\na condition called face blindness.\n\n27\n00:01:37,898 --> 00:01:39,537\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n28\n00:01:39,894 --> 00:01:41,497\nExpert seeing is learned.\n\n29\n00:01:42,039 --> 00:01:45,067\nFluent readers see familiar letter groups at a glance,\n\n30\n00:01:45,270 --> 00:01:49,356\nbecause their visual brain has been trained on thousands of words.\n\n31\n00:01:49,820 --> 00:01:51,912\nBeginning readers are still building that,\n\n32\n00:01:52,132 --> 00:01:54,778\nso give them time and plenty of practice.\n\n33\n00:01:56,948 --> 00:01:59,650\nYour visual brain learns what it sees most.\n\n34\n00:01:59,961 --> 00:02:03,668\nFor readers, words became as familiar as faces.\n\n"
